  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROTECTION OF MARINE-COASTAL RESOURCES AND IMPROVEMENT OF THE LIVELIHOODS OF THE PACIFIC OF GUATEMALA FISHING COMMUNITIES</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION EFFORTS TO PROTECT SHARK SPECIES, HEREIN, SHARKS, SKATES, AND RAYS, ARE OF PARAMOUNT IMPORTANCE. THE IUCN RED LISTS SHARK SPECIALIST GROUP ESTIMATES THAT 24 OF ALL EXTANT SHARKS AND CHIMERAS ARE THREATENED WITH AN ELEVATED RISK OF EXTINCTION. THE UTILITY OF SHARK PRODUCTS IS DRIVING TARGETED AND OPPORTUNISTIC SHARK FISHING. EXPOSURE TO FISHING MORTALITIES AND LOW INTRINSIC RATES OF POPULATION GROWTH MEAN THERE IS A CRITICALNEED FOR EFFECTIVE SHARK CONSERVATION MEASURES.THE LACK OF INFORMATION IN CENTRAL AMERICA, DUE TO THE ABSENCE OF DATA COLLECTION EFFORTS, IS WORRISOME. THEREARE MANY INFORMATION GAPS IN MONITORING AND DATA COLLECTING IN THE REGION. THIS ABSENCE OF INFORMATION MAKES ITDIFFICULT TO IMPLEMENT SOUND REGULATIONS IN CHONDRICHTHYES FISHERIES. IN GUATEMALA, LONGLINE FISHERIES TARGET THECATCH OF SHARKS AND RAYS, BUT SPECIES ARE ALSO CAPTURED INCIDENTALLY, THROUGH OTHER FISHERIES, WHICH AREPERMITTED BOTH THE PACIFIC AND CARIBBEAN COASTS.THE CHONDRICHTHYES FISHERIES AND TRADE IN GUATEMALA ARE, AT THIS MOMENT, UNREGULATED AND UNREPORTED. THERE IS NO KNOWLEDGE ABOUT THE FISHING PRESSURE OF THE RESOURCE, THERE IS NO TRACEABILITY OF THE PRODUCTS AND BY-PRODUCTS,ALSO, THERE IS NO DATA ON THE DEMAND OF PRODUCTS, MAINLY OF FINS, IN DOMESTIC MARKETS AND THE INTERNATIONALTRADE.</narrative>
  </description>
